.TH XYMONAPI 8 "October 30, 2026" "xymon-api"
.SH NAME
xymonapi \- read-only HTTP and JSON interface to the Xymon server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B "$XYMONHOME/ext/xymonapi/xymonapi.sh"

.SH DESCRIPTION
xymonapi answers HTTP requests with what the Xymon server knows: the status
board, the alarms on it, its hosts, the values in its RRD files and the
graphs drawn from them, and the history of colour changes with the status
text saved at each. Answers are JSON, except for graphs, which are PNG.
It changes nothing in Xymon: it only reads.

It is optional, and shipped disabled. It is started by
.I xymonlaunch(8)
from the task file \fBtasks.d/xymonapi\fR beside tasks.cfg, which its
installation creates with a DISABLED line. To run it, remove that line;
xymonlaunch starts it once xymond is running, and restarts it if it stops.

It needs a Xymon built from its current main branch: its settings go in
xymonserver.d/, which no release of Xymon reads yet. On 4.3.30 a file there
is ignored, password included, and xymonapi then answers anyone.

Unlike the rest of Xymon, xymonapi needs Python 3.10 or later, with the
modules fastapi, uvicorn and yaml (requirements.txt beside it lists them),
importable by the user Xymon runs as. When one is missing it logs which, and
exits with status 77.

The OpenAPI description of every operation is served at /openapi.json, and
is the file openapi.yaml beside the program.

.SH OPERATIONS
Every path is under /xymon/api/v1. Only GET is accepted, but for POST and
DELETE on /tokens, and any other path answers 404.
.IP "\fB/health\fR"
Whether xymond answers a ping: {"alive":true,"server_version":"xymond ..."}
when it does, and {"alive":false} when the ping fails -- xymond is down, or
XYMSRV or XYMON is wrong. Like every path, it answers 401 without a user, or
a client token, when XYMONAPI_PASSWD is set.
Its "search" field describes the index /search reads -- how many passages
of manual pages and of history, when it was built -- and is null when there
is none.
.IP "\fB/states\fR [\fBentity\fR=HOST] [\fBtest\fR=TEST] [\fBverdict\fR=VERDICT]"
The status board, one state per host and test. The colour is given as a
verdict: green is ok, yellow warning, red critical, blue disabled, clear
nodata and purple unknown.
.IP "\fB/alarms\fR"
Every state whose verdict is not ok.
.IP "\fB/entities\fR"
Each host on the board, once.
.IP "\fB/status\fR \fBentity\fR=HOST \fBtest\fR=TEST"
The current status of HOST's TEST, from xymondboard: its verdict, when it
last changed (time) and was last reported (updated), and its text -- what
the web page shows, written by the monitored host or the test. When it is
acknowledged -- until the acknowledgement's end time -- or the test disabled
-- by hand, or blue from a DOWNTIME tag in hosts.cfg -- that is said too,
with the words given and until when. The command line that set the status
is left out of the text, as the web page leaves it out. HOST and TEST are
taken as given, not as patterns: 404 when the board has no such status. At
most 1 MiB of text.
.IP "\fB/search\fR \fBq\fR=QUESTION [\fBkind\fR=docs|history] [\fBk\fR=N]"
The N passages (5 by default, 20 at most) of Xymon's manual pages and of
its history nearest QUESTION by meaning, best first, from the index that
.I xymonapi-rag(1)
builds: the task tasks.d/xymonapi-rag, once a day. Each passage says where
it comes from -- page, section and item, or host, test and time -- and a
history passage carries the status text the monitored host sent, marked as
data, not instructions. The index is read again when the file changes, so
the task needs no restart of the API. 404, saying so, when there is no index
or the modules in rag/requirements.txt are missing: search is optional, and
the other operations need neither.
.IP "\fB/client\fR [\fBname\fR=NAME] [\fBformat\fR=sh|env]"
A setup script for the machine that asks -- a laptop -- with this API's
address and the user who asked written in: it fetches the client code at
the commit this API was installed from (recorded by make install in a git
checkout; the newest otherwise), runs make install-client, makes a
virtualenv with the mcp module, checks it reaches this API and trusts its
certificate, asks for the password once to get the machine a token (see
TOKENS) and keeps the token, not the password, checks /health answers, and
registers the MCP server with Claude Code, when it is installed, under
NAME (xymon by default; one name per Xymon server). To be read,
then run. The address is XYMONAPI_PUBLIC_URL when set, else the one the
request came through: the web server's, with X-Forwarded-Host and
X-Forwarded-Proto, as xymonapi-apache.conf sends them. The password is
never in it. format=env gives the three settings alone.
.IP "\fB/series\fR \fBentity\fR=HOST \fBtest\fR=TEST [\fBmetric\fR=DATASET] [\fBfrom\fR=TIME] [\fBto\fR=TIME]"
The values of one dataset of the RRD file TEST.rrd of HOST, under
$XYMONRRDS, read with rrdtool. TEST is the file's name, not the column on
the status board: xymond_rrd names the files after the data it keeps, so
the conn test is in tcp.conn.rrd and the ssh test in tcp.ssh.rrd, cpu load
is in la.rrd and memory in memory.rrd, and a disk is in disk,<mount with
commas for slashes>.rrd. DATASET is a column in that file: lambda is the
load average in la.rrd, sec the response time in a tcp.*.rrd. DATASET
defaults to lambda, and a missing file or dataset is answered 404; from
after to is a 400. The file is read as it is on disk, and xymond_rrd
writes it every 30 minutes unless run with --no-cache (or a graph is shown,
which has the cache written first): the newest points can be that late, and
a null at the end is not a test that stopped. To see
what a host has, list $XYMONRRDS/HOST/ there, or run rrdtool info on a file.
.IP "\fB/graphs\fR \fBentity\fR=HOST \fBtest\fR=TEST [\fBfrom\fR=TIME] [\fBto\fR=TIME]"
The graph
.I showgraph.cgi(1)
draws for HOST and TEST, fetched from showgraph.sh, its wrapper, at
$XYMONSERVERCGIURL -- prefixed with $XYMONWEBHOST when that is a path, as
xymonserver.cfg ships it.
.IP "\fB/events\fR [\fBentity\fR=HOST] [\fBtest\fR=TEST] [\fBsince\fR=TIME] [\fBlimit\fR=N] [\fBbefore\fR=SECONDS]"
The colour changes xymond_history recorded in $XYMONHISTDIR/allevents, the
newest first: when, the verdict before and after, and how long the one
before had lasted. N is 1 to 1000, 100 by default. With before, only the
changes older than that time: the epoch of an answer's last item asks for
the page after it. The file is read from its end, so the newest changes
cost the same however long the history. A server where nothing has changed
colour yet has none.
.IP "\fB/histlog\fR \fBentity\fR=HOST \fBtest\fR=TEST \fBtime\fR=SECONDS"
The status text xymond_history saved under $XYMONHISTLOGS when HOST's TEST
changed colour at that time, the epoch of an /events item; 404 when none was
saved. At most 1 MiB of it.
.LP
A TIME is \fBnow\fR, seconds since the epoch, or a time relative to now such
as \fB-30m\fR, \fB-24h\fR, \fB-7d\fR or \fB-2w\fR. \fBfrom\fR defaults to
-24h and \fBto\fR to now. A host or test name that is not one Xymon uses,
or a time it cannot read, is answered with 400. A test name may hold a
colon, as an HTTP test's RRD file does (tcp.http.www.example.com:8080,);
a host name may not.

.SH TOKENS
With XYMONAPI_PASSWD set, a user may get a token for one machine and send it
instead of the password: Authorization: Bearer TOKEN. The setup script that
/client writes does so, naming the machine by its short hostname, so the
password stays off the laptop. A token opens every path but /tokens, which
takes the password: a stolen token cannot make others, or renew itself.
.IP "\fBPOST /tokens\fR {"client": NAME}"
A new token for the caller on NAME -- letters, digits, . _ or -, 64 at most
-- replacing any the caller had there, which is refused from then on. It is
shown this once: only its SHA-256 is kept, in $XYMONAPI_TOKENS.
.IP "\fBGET /tokens\fR"
The caller's tokens: each machine's name and when its token was made.
.IP "\fBDELETE /tokens/\fRNAME"
Revokes the caller's token on NAME.
.LP
A user sees and revokes only their own tokens. An admin revokes anyone's by
removing that line from $XYMONAPI_TOKENS: one JSON object per token, with
its user, machine and SHA-256. The machine's name is the laptop's word, not
proof: it names the token, and two machines of one name share one.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
The task file loads xymonserver.cfg, and xymonapi uses these settings from it:
.IP "\fBXYMSRV\fR"
The Xymon server to ask. It is required; xymonapi does not fall back to
127.0.0.1.
.IP "\fBXYMON\fR"
The xymon client program.
.IP "\fBXYMONRRDS\fR"
Where the RRD files are, for /series.
.IP "\fBXYMONSERVERCGIURL\fR, \fBXYMONWEBHOST\fR"
Where showgraph.cgi is, for /graphs. The first is usually a path, /xymon-cgi;
the second, http://$XYMONSERVERWWWNAME, then gives the host, and must carry
the port when the web server is not on 80.
.IP "\fBXYMONHISTDIR\fR, \fBXYMONHISTLOGS\fR"
Where xymond_history keeps the events and the saved status texts, for
/events and /histlog.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_PUBLIC_URL\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_REPO\fR"
What /client writes: the address clients reach this API at, and where the
client code is fetched from, https://github.com/bonomani/xymon-api by
default. Set XYMONAPI_PUBLIC_URL on a server other machines reach: without
it the address is the one the request carried, in its Host or
X-Forwarded-Host header, which is the requester's word, and the script
says so in its first lines.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_RAG_INDEX\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_RAG_MODEL\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_RAG_OFFLINE\fR"
The index /search reads, and the embedding model, as for xymonapi-rag(1),
which writes the index with the same settings from the same file.
.LP
Seven settings are its own. Put them in a file under
\fBxymonserver.d/\fR beside xymonserver.cfg, which reads its files, for
instance xymonapi.cfg:
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_LISTEN\fR"
The address and port to listen on, as HOST:PORT or [ADDRESS]:PORT for IPv6.
The default is 127.0.0.1:1986, which only this machine reaches.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_PASSWD\fR"
An htpasswd file: every request needs a user and password from it, and is
answered 401 otherwise. The default is Xymon's own,
$XYMONHOME/etc/xymonpasswd, when it exists -- the file the web pages use,
whose users useradm.cgi and chpasswd.cgi manage -- so the API asks for
what the pages ask for with nothing set. Set it to another file, or to the
empty string for an API open to anyone who can reach it. The password is
checked by Apache's htpasswd program, which must be on PATH; xymonapi does
not start if it is not, or if it cannot read the file.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_TOKENS\fR"
The file of client tokens (TOKENS above), $XYMONVAR/xymonapi/tokens by
default. The API creates it, mode 600, in a directory of mode 700.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_TLS_CERT\fR, \fBXYMONAPI_TLS_KEY\fR"
A certificate and its key, PEM: with them the API speaks HTTPS on its port,
and plain HTTP gets no answer. The defaults are xymond's own,
$XYMONHOME/etc/tls/xymond.pem and xymond.key as
.I xymon-tls(7)
sets them up, so a server with TLS for the Xymon protocol has it for the
API too, one certificate for both. Without the files the API speaks plain
HTTP. A certificate without its key, or a key that others can read, stops
it from starting. Clients that do not trust the certificate name it, or
the CA that signed it, in XYMONAPI_CA (xymonapi-mcp(1)).
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_HIDE\fR"
A file of regular expressions, one per line, # for a comment. Whatever
they match is replaced by [hidden] in every text the API returns: a
status, an acknowledgement or disabling, a saved status -- and so in the
search index built from them. A process list can show a password typed on
a command line; a line such as \fBpassword=\\S+\fR keeps it off the API,
whatever the web page shows. Read at start: an expression that is not one,
or a file that cannot be read, stops the API from starting, since running
without the hiding asked for would be worse than not running.
.IP "\fBXYMONAPI_PYTHON\fR"
The Python to run the API with, when the modules it needs are not installed
for the system's python3: the python3 of a virtualenv that holds them, for
instance. The default is python3, found on PATH.

.SH FILES
.IP "\fB$XYMONHOME/ext/xymonapi/\fR"
The program: xymonapi.sh, the launcher the task runs; app.py and xymon.py,
the server; openapi.yaml, the description served at /openapi.json; and
requirements.txt, the Python modules it needs.
.IP "\fB$XYMONHOME/etc/tasks.d/xymonapi\fR"
The xymonlaunch task, installed with a DISABLED line. A later installation
leaves it as it is.
.IP "\fB$XYMONHOME/etc/xymonapi-apache.conf\fR, \fBxymonapi-nginx.conf\fR, \fBxymonapi-lighttpd.conf\fR"
Snippets that serve the API through the web server, for other machines:
/xymon/api/ passed to 127.0.0.1:1986, in the HTTPS part where the server
has one. Include the one for your web server where its Xymon configuration
is. Installed once; a later installation leaves them as they are.
.IP "\fB$XYMONHOME/etc/xymonserver.d/\fR"
A file of yours here holds the settings of its own listed under
ENVIRONMENT. Nothing is installed there.
.IP "\fB$XYMONVAR/xymonapi/tokens\fR"
The client tokens' SHA-256s, one JSON line each (TOKENS above).
.IP "\fB$XYMONSERVERLOGS/xymonapi.log\fR"
The log, written through the task's LOGFILE. A missing Python or module is
reported here, and so is each token made, revoked, or used: user@machine.
.LP
$XYMONHOME/etc and $XYMONHOME/ext are links to wherever an installation keeps
those directories, /etc/xymon for instance.

.SH NOTES
A failure of the server's own -- xymond not answering, rrdtool missing --
is a 502. Its answer names the reason when the text is xymonapi's own, as
"XYMONRRDS is not set"; a program's error text, which can hold paths and
settings, goes to the log instead, and the answer says so.

xymonapi needs nothing central -- no directory, no certificate authority
it must reach, no name service -- so that it answers when the rest of the
network is in trouble: Xymon is what one looks at then. Its password file
and its certificate are Xymon's own, files on the server.

From other machines, use HTTPS: with xymond's certificate the API speaks it
itself (XYMONAPI_TLS_CERT), and where the web server has a certificate the
snippets under FILES pass /xymon/api/ to the API over it. An SSH tunnel to
127.0.0.1:1986 is encrypted too, with nothing to set up. Plain HTTP across
a network carries the password and the status texts readable, as port 1984
does; keep it to one machine, or to a network where the Xymon pages
themselves are plain and trusted. Either way, what must not leak are
secrets: the password file, which only the Xymon user reads, the key, and
what XYMONAPI_HIDE blanks out of the texts.

Requests are served from a thread pool: a slow one holds up itself alone.

.SH "SEE ALSO"
xymonlaunch(8), tasks.cfg(5), xymonserver.cfg(5), xymon(1), showgraph.cgi(1),
xymon(7), xymon-tls(7), xymonapi-mcp(1), xymonapi-rag(1)
